  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/25f-perchlike-fishes-6-gobies/schismatogobius_ampluvinculus_en/",
      "html": "<p>A contrasting black-and-white goby from Taiwan. Every Schismatogobius has an individual coloration: no two specimens look exactly alike, and they can change their colour spectacularly. Available size: 2–5 cm.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/25f-perchlike-fishes-6-gobies/schismatogobius_ampluvinculus_en/",
      "html": "<p>Taiwan.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/25f-perchlike-fishes-6-gobies/schismatogobius_ampluvinculus_en/",
      "html": "<p>Adults of Schismatogobius are restricted to fresh water. They usually live in larger groups in shallow parts of running water bodies. When disturbed they bury themselves very fast by wriggling movements. S. ampluvinculus is known to inhabit pebbly ground.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/25f-perchlike-fishes-6-gobies/schismatogobius_ampluvinculus_en/",
      "html": "<p>S. ampluvinculus is a subtropical species and should therefore be kept under temperate conditions.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/25f-perchlike-fishes-6-gobies/schismatogobius_ampluvinculus_en/",
      "html": "<p>Feeding is unproblematic: they readily take frozen and live food.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "reproduction",
      "source": "glaser",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.aquariumglaser.de/en/25-perchlike-fishes/25f-perchlike-fishes-6-gobies/schismatogobius_ampluvinculus_en/",
      "html": "<p>So far no successful breeding report has appeared for any of the about 10 Schismatogobius species, although they spawn quite readily. The extremely tiny larvae probably develop only in a marine environment.</p>"
    }
  ],
